Date: Wed, 12 May 93 11:16:19 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Meta: Privatizing Judges II

I argued:
> But unless private judges issue verdicts that tend to cost more to
> enforce, the PPL issue is independent of the charging issue.

Harry Shapiro responded:
> If there is more of them or the punishments more time consuming to
> enforce, than there is a real cost diff.

Let's review.  If two people have a dispute, and use the LA, then the
LA must review the evidence, render a verdict, and then enforce that
verdict.  If they use a private judge, the LA might never hear about
the dispute.  However, sometimes private judges would request the LA
to help enforce some verdict, and in some of those cases the person
labeled guilty might dispute that the private judge had juristiction. 
In these few cases, the LA might have to review evidence regarding
prior contracts, and render a verdict regarding juristiction.  

Judging contracts sounds easier on average than judging other
offenses.  So imagine that people with private judges tended to have
disputes twice as often, and that private judges tended to issue
verdicts that were twice as hard to enforce.   If less than 25% of
private cases involved disputed juristiction, that would still mean a
lower total workload for the LA!  So while an increased LA workload is
possible, it seems unlikely.  

Therefore, why not wait till a real problem arises before trying to
fix it?  Allow private judges, and if it really seems that disputant
pairs using private judges cost the LA more trouble on average than
others, then we could consider further measures.

I wrote further:
> Regarding costs and charging, it would seem that if the LA is going to
> charge to implement private verdicts, he/she should also charge to
> implement LA list verdicts.  And if implementation is charged for, why
> not have the LA also charge for doing their judging.  This would
> especially encourage a PPL of possibly cheaper judges.  

Harry responded further:
> ExI judgments are a service of the list. Private judgments maybe 
> better, faster, or in other ways better for indivual list members
> and as such are probally worth some premium.

Given that one thing is a service, and the other not, then the one
might be free and the other charged for.  But the question here is
what *should* the service of the list be, not what we think it *is*.

Robin Hanson

