Date: Wed, 12 May 1993 14:45:36 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Meta: Privatizing Judges II

After arguing that the List Administrator's burdens will probably not
increase if he/she occasionally enforces private adjudication contracts,
Robin says:

>Therefore, why not wait till a real problem arises before trying to
>fix it?  Allow private judges, and if it really seems that disputant
>pairs using private judges cost the LA more trouble on average than
>others, then we could consider further measures.

I agree that this is a very reasonable approach.  Codifying it would, I
think, call for giving the LA discretion to enter into reasonable and
appropriate agreements with third party adjudicators to enforce their
judgements.  IMO the default rule should adopt a minimalist approach:
charging nothing and taking people at their word that they have agreed to
an adjudicator's decision.  The LA should also start out by simply
enforcing the adjudicator's decision.

If the LA faces more burdens under this policy than prior to its adoption
(an unlikely event), then the LA could start charging for enforcement. 
Note that some sort of equilibrium should come about, for as the LA begins
to charge for enforcing the decisions of private adjudicators, people will
start to simply ask the LA to take on the burdens of deciding and enforcing
cases him/herself, for "free".  The LA thus has an incentive to keep
enforcement charges low or nonexistent.

If asked to enforce a decision that grossly violates list policies, I think
the LA should simply decline to do so.  The whole policy of contracting out
adjudication loses its benefits if the LA starts meddling in the specifics
of slightly off-whack decisions.  But, in any case, the LA and third party
adjudicators could work out other systems of review if want to work around
this default rule.

