Date: Wed, 12 May 93 17:15:40 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: AI: Synaptic Storage Requirements

Derek Zahn writes:
>
>1) Unrealistic.  Well, that's a damning criticism if true -- but any
>such criticism is quite welcome because presumably it is backed by
>a reason, which can then hopefully be used to make the estimates more
>realistic.
>

Neural network researchers, including myself, make certain assumptions in order
to begin to build an understanding.  This is unavoidable.  However, to
confidently assume that our assumptions are correct and to begin calculating
conclusions based upon them is precarious.  It is this that I'm pointing out -
that we cannot make quantitative statements until we understand qualitatively
what we are counting.  I'm claiming that we don't have this understanding yet.

For example, the "activation" of a neuron usually refers to an average firing
frequency.  However, as someone pointed out, the phase relationship between the
spike trains of two neurons is crucial to determining whether they will elicit
an action potential in a third post-synaptic neuron.  This phase information is
ignored in many models (including Grossberg's).  This assumption may not be
terribly wrong, since neurons are noisy devices and probably do not keep
precise phase relationships - and perhaps a statistical simplification is
accurate (Though some systems _do_ seem to keep careful phase
relationships...).  Another common assumption is that the effect of
pre-synaptic neurons on a post-synaptic cell is a summation of their
activations times the weights on their connections.  This is something I feel
comfortable with, but it makes neurophysiologists shudder.  I can imagine many
other relationships - for example, if two different neurotransmitters are
required to activate a post-synaptic cell and each is released by one of two
pre-synaptic cells, then the effect is more like a product of the activations,
not a sum.  A third assumption is that the "weight" on a synapse can be
represented as a single number, lumping all the nasty variables I mentioned in
a previous message into one final transmission gating strength.  This ignores
transmitter depletion, non-specific diffusive signals (which _are_ important,
BTW, and some people do include them in their models), and a number of chemical
and perhaps even genetic factors.

Again, I am guilty of all these assumptions.  We have to start somewhere.
However, I prefer not to be guilty of forgetting what assumptions I have made
and extrapolating in a vaccuum.

>
>2) Uninformative.  By this, are you just repeating the "inaccurate"
>implied by the first objection, or do you think that the answers
>(if accurate) don't mean anything?  Why would you say that?
>

Let's assume for a moment that we can base a qualitative conclusion on our
admittedly precarious calculations.  The question we ask: "Can we fit a brain
into a modern computer?" can have two answers, yes or no.

If the answer is yes, what do we get?  We still don't know how to represent the
information in the brain so that it assumes its functional qualities.  We still
don't know how to retrieve that information, how to activate it etc.  We may
say the hardware is there but no software has been written.

If the answer is no, then we are tempted to call for larger and larger
machines.  "If I could just have some more terabytes, I just know it will
work..."  But again without an understanding of the principles we can't say
that with faster and bigger computers we can solve the problem.  Until the
theoretical breakthrough are made the technological ones won't do us any good.

Again, my point is that we can't make quantitative calculations until we know
what we are quantifying.  If we do, our results are likely to be meaningless.
Consider again Drake's equation - what did we gain after we completed the
calculation?

>> A related question:  "How big of a balloon is needed to go to the Moon?"
>
>Ah -- now I see, maybe.  That question doesn't have any answer.*
>Are you implying that there IS no answer to the question of how
>much computational resources would be required for an adequate
>simulation of a brain?  Why?

No, what I'm saying is that the brothers Montgolfier (sp?) may have argued that
since bigger balloons are needed to go higher, we can get to the moon if only
we construct a large enough balloon.  They would be wrong because they would be
extrapolating upon limited knowledge.  I am making a comparison to current
theories of mind and consciousness...

(However, Kent Hastings made a very clever suggestion that shows the weakness
of my analogy.  Want to work in neural nets Kent?  ;-)

>
>So if we're interested in the properties of neurons, we are naturally
>led to the question of how many important bits are necessary
>to describe a neuron's instantaneous state?  That is, how many bits
>are required to specify its functional properties from among the
>various possibilities in a sufficient class of neuron models.

Why bits?  Let's consider for a moment how NN work is done.  We simulate the
activity of large groups of neurons by doing linear algebra, solving non-linear
differential equations, etc.  To do this we use large computers composed of
things like capacitors, resistors, transistors, etc. that give us binary logic,
assembly languages, Mathematica, etc.  Connection machines are pretty similar,
still operating on a digital principle.  However, consider the
neuron-equivalent circuit, which is a simple abstraction of a neuron membrane
using some resistors, a capacitor, a battery, and some pieces of wire (I'm
oversimplifying).  Using cable theory, we can construct a pretty good model of
a dendritic tree out of these, then add things like voltage-gated resistors,
current sources...  With lots of these we can build a pretty good neuron, much
simpler than the digital technology normally used to simulate the same thing.
(Of course, this model also makes some dangerous assumptions - like leaving out
channel specificity to particular ions...)

But I'm not arguing for analog VLSI.  I prefer digital simulations because the
technology is more entrenched.  However, why do we have to limit ourselves to
it?  "When the only tool you have is a hammer, every problem looks like a nail"
(I forget who posted that quote, but it's quite appropriate)

If you're talking about bits in an information theoretic sense, then again I
ask what the necessary information is?  Synaptic connections?  Transmitter
levels?  Dendritic structure?  Glial cells (ack!)?  We can abstract and
simplify, but the longer your chain of assumptions the more likely it is that
your conclusions are wrong.

So we want to talk about uploading - fine.  My question is why do we always
start discussing this in terms of computer architectures?  Somebody asked how
many 8mm tapes to use for backup.  I'd suggest zero - don't start your
collection yet...  ;-)  It's tempting and comforting to assume that we
understand something.  It's ok to start speculating on AI and uploading and
FTL travel, but let's remember that this is only speculation.  Let's not
start quantifying our conclusions and writing expected milestones on our
calendars.  It's important to know what we don't know (and to keep it in
mind...)

-Paul


