From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: LIFE-X: the 120 yr limit, cryogenics, blah

Peter McCluskey writes:

>
> If gain control in neurons works anything like gain control works in
>signal processing hardware, then having a group of 5 or more neurons
>which can discriminate 9 orders of magnitude is completely consistent
>with 7-bit accuracy for individual neurons.

Does the scheme you mention use each neuron to represent a portion of the
mantissa?  Like using several bytes to build a longword?  ...or do I
misunderstand you?  Gain control in neural systems (according to some theories)
works by representing relative values only and discarding any absolute
information.  For many tasks (especially visual ones) this is apparently
sufficient and even desirable.  Linking cells through lateral inhibition
allows them to store such relative values, and can be accomplished with as
few as two neurons...

But your point is well taken, both schemes only require a little accuracy in
the activation level of each neuron.  But this is a steady state value, the
things that determine the behavior over time (like dendritic structure, chemical
concentrations, etc.) may be too complicated to store in 7-bits.  Presumably it
is the long-term memory we are more interested in storing anyway...

>
> As for the other ways you mentioned that a neuron could store information,
>they are conceivable, but until I hear a theory of how their long term
>state could be "learned" in response to sensory input, I will assume they
>are unimportant to information storage.
>

Why would you assume this?  Unless by "learning" you also mean "development".
I'd assume we want to store all the functional characteristics...
Besides, non-specific effects can be learned - for example, the number of
dopamine receptors is controlled by, among other things, dopamine
concentration.  This "learning" is on a longer time scale, but it does seem to
be of behavioral significance.

Anyway, synaptic "weights" are presumably learned by a Hebbian correlation, an
assumption most people accept.  However, the mechanism for the Hebbian synapse
has still not been found (though recent findings suggest that carbon monoxide,
of all things, may be involved...)

-Paul


