Try this experiment instead:

1) With a bucket of sand, held with a rope, spin at a constant rate.
   (if you prefer, use a toy car and a string in your driveway).

2) Now slowly let some rope out. DO NOT INCREASE YOUR SPIN RATE.
   (remember the Earth spins at a constant rate)

3) What happened?

Your fingertips are at the "GEO equivalent" in this demonstration.

Marc> Mainly, I'm puzzled why you think in terms of angular momentum rather than
Marc> gravitational forces.  As a check on your reasoning, do you get the same
Marc> answer when you move the elevator quasi-statically (that is, very very
Marc> slowly)?

Yes.

It just means that "less" lateral force is induced into the
"tether/tower" as the elevator ascends.  Once the elevator has stopped
no additional lateral force (ignoring cosmic wind) is induced into the
"tether/tower" and the "upper"  tower is in tension.

So to summarize:
When the elevator is rising-- It's a tower (receiving a lateral thrust).
When the elevator is stopped (above GEO) -- It's a tether (under tension
