I will now make my usual point about speculations on "what year will X
be possible", as applied brain capacity/brain simulation speculation.

Lets say that our estimates are off by a factor of one hundred --
thats two orders of magnitude. What will this likely mean in terms of
how far off our estimate of what year a computer of particular power
is available? Well, computers are speeding up a factor of one hundred
every ten years or so. We therefore can note that given the other
likely fudges involved a mere two orders of magnitude would be lost in
the noise.

To me, it matters little if uploading is practical in thirty years or
in fifty years or in seventy. Well, it matters a wee bit -- that might
dictate whether I "make it" to the singularity under my own power
(that is, without cryonics). However, in general, speculations about
"when will we have a human equivalent computer" are best answered
"approximately within our lifetimes". More than that isn't terribly
necessary unless you are trying to make money on the idea futures
market. Yes, I can see how this might be a topic of practical interest
to SOMEONE, but in general, isn't the fact that we are going to get
there within a few measly decades sufficient?

