HEADLINE: Letter to Clinton; OPPOSITION MOUNTS TO 
PROPOSED GOVT.  CLIPPER CHIP  ENCRYPTION STANDARD

BODY:

Secret govt. encryption standard unveiled by White House 4 weeks 
ago in form of " Clipper Chip"  (CD April 19 p2) is being opposed by 
decade-old coalition of nation's largest computer and 
telecommunications companies known as Digital Privacy & Security 
Working Group. Group wrote President Clinton Thurs. in which they 
"applaud" Administration's efforts to "develop a greater 
understanding" of complex encryption/privacy issues, but took issue 
with  Clipper Chip  plan, questioning its impact on "fundamental 
privacy and other constitutional rights . . . when any domestic 
surveillance scheme is proposed." It said it also was unsure how 
proposal would affect U.S. export controls.

Govt.'s plan is to have new encryption data-encoding scheme 
embedded in Clipper Chips  that then are installed in various devices. 
Encryption could be broken (wiretapped) only if 3 separate "keys" are 
used. Users of devices would hold one key and 2 govt.-appointed 
agencies or organizations each would hold one of 2 other keys in 
"escrow accounts," according to plan introduced in April by National 
Institute of Standards & Technology (NIST). On court order, law 
enforcement agencies would gain access to 2 govt.-held keys, which 
then could be used in tandem to decode encrypted conversations.

Clipper Chip  plan, which was started during Bush Administration, 
was embraced by Clinton Administration. However, it now has drawn 
criticism from host of civil libertarian groups and computer and 
telecommunications officials who claim plan is inherently flawed 
because computer program used to encrypt signals is classified top 
secret and isn't open to public inspection. That secrecy, groups charge, 
leaves several questions as to whether there might be secret 
"backdoors" written into code to which only top-level govt. agencies, 
such as National Security Agency (NSA), would have access. 
Backdoors also would allow NSA to bypass govt. escrow key account 
system because they could tap into conversation at any time, critics 
said. But officials of NIST and NSA repeatedly have denied existence 
of any such backdoor.

Group's letter to Clinton, signed by AT&T, Digital Equipment, 
Electronic Frontier Founda tion (EFF), IBM, McCaw Cellular, MCI and 
USTA, among others, questioned secrecy of program. EFF Washington 
Dir. Jerry Berman said group is urging Administration to examine 
policy more closely, urging "detailed debate" on issue. Among 
questions letter raised: (1) Whether key escrow system will produce 
desired law enforcement results. (2) Level of strength and integrity of 
algorithm (backdoor issue). (3) Practicality and commercial 
acceptability of program. (4) Effect of proposal on American 
competitiveness and balance of trade.

Presence of AT&T as signatory to letter is notable because it was first 
company to state publicly that it would be installing  Clipper Chip  in 
device already being sold to govt. Justice Dept. has ordered "several 
thousand" of AT&T

Clipper Chip -equipped devices, Justice Dept. spokesman said. AT&T 
doesn't oppose plan, but said it's seeking clarification on several issues 
involving encryption policy.

Clipper Chip  program also has picked up opposition in Congress, 
where House Telecom Subcommittee Chmn. Markey (D-Mass.) has 
promised to hold hearings on issue and role NSA should be playing in 
setting encryption standards. Markey and those signing letter to 
Clinton pointed out that several publicly available encryption 
standards are in use today and could continue to be used, thus 
bypassing govt.'s standard, which White House has said will remain 
"voluntary." Markey said NSA is "trying to put the cryptography genie 
back in the bottle" when various encryption methods already are open 
to public.

