SECTION: Vol. 13, No. 87; Pg. 2

LENGTH: 1179 words

HEADLINE: Membership of 5-15 Likely; ADMINISTRATION EAGER 
TO LAUNCH INFORMATION INFRASTRUCTURE TASK FORCE

BODY:

White House-level Information Infrastructure Task Force will get 
under way as soon as Senate completes confirmation process for 
several key players, with membership likely to include 5-15 senior 
Clinton Administration officials and with separate issue subgroups 
operating somewhat independently. Panel was promised in Feb. as 
top-level coordinating body by President Clinton and Vice President 
Gore in Silicon Valley announcement of infrastructure plan (CD Feb 23 
p3). Michael Nelson, senior adviser in Office of Science & Technology 
Policy (OSTP), in luncheon address to National Information 
Infrastructure seminar in Washington suburb of Arlington, Va., Tues., 
said "the main thing is to provide leadership." Key reason for 
convening task force is to address concerns on international policies 
and trade matters, as well as to centralize domestic policymaking, he 
said. Symposium was co-hosted by USA Information Exchange and 
Institute of Electronics & Electrical Engineers (IEEE).

Industry advisory groups also will be formed by subject topic, Nelson 
said, but Administration remains "troubled" by ethics restrictions that 
would tend to limit participation of senior corporate officials, who are 
best suited to give govt. advice. "These advisory commissions will 
work with the high-level group as a sounding board," Nelson said. 
Goal is to establish policy and dominance within Congress, FCC, 
Administration. "If we get business on our side, we've got the 
advantage," he told audience, composed primarily of industry officials.

Delay in forming Task Force has been caused by need to have top-level 
officials in place, especially Deputy Commerce Secy. John Rollwagen 
and NTIA Administrator Larry Irving, who await Senate confirmation 
hearings. Administration official told us OSTP Dir. John Gibbons and 
Robert Ruvin, of Economic Policy Council, are likely to convene top-
level meeting to work out who will chair panel. Identity of chmn. will 
have impact on operational direction, official said, declining to 
speculate on who it would be. "There's still a lot of work to be done, 
but we have an outline," official said.

On other issues, Nelson said: (1) It's not clear that  Clipper Chip,  
encrypted computer chip that would allow law enforcement access to 
telecommunications, would become govt. standard because of 
competing ideas in private sector. He said it wasn't true that govt. was 
trying to force all users to adopt encrypted chip. (2) Administration 
hasn't taken position on whether RHCs should be allowed into 
information services market through lifting of MFJ restrictions. He said 
he agreed with MFJ legislation filed in 1986 by Sen. Breaux (D-La.) that 
he described as saying "I don't care what policy you have. We need a 
policy. So let's have a policy." (3) State PUCs, with differing policies, 
can admit or block creative ideas, condition that may indicate need for 
centralized national policy guidelines, he said. At present, Nelson said, 
they're proving grounds, but PUCs, like industry, are looking for 
national leadership. Asked whether he would go so far as to establish 
national policy that would eliminate jurisdiction of 50 PUCs, Nelson 
deflected question: "The more interesting question is what do you do 
about the 13,000 jurisdictions that oversee cable."

Calling "infostructure" revolution "the new story line dominating the 
world" in aftermath of fall of communism, House Telecom 
Subcommittee Chmn. Markey (D-Mass.) said America must 
concentrate on developing telecommunications program that will 
build "on and off ramps to consumers." Goal, he said, is to make 
services easier to use and more affordable under framework of central, 
national policy with clear goals. Markey said in speech to symposium 
Wed. that "technology convergence," disappearance of separate TV, 
radio, cellular, telephone, cable and fax systems, represented "a vast, 
ultracompetitive multimedia marketplace" that industry must be ready 
to enter.

"This is not bumper sticker material, it's not a subject that will make 
the cover of Time," Markey said, outlining long-term goals that he said 
should include next-generation wireless technology and new wireline 
equipment, such as broadband, digital, enhanced interoperability and 
development of network of networks. In short term, he said, Plain-Old-
Telephone Service (POTS) "should evolve into PODS," Plain-Old-
Digital-Service. "Unless we move to PODS, we will divide our society 
along the lines of rich and poor," Markey said. He challenged Japanese 
doctrine that contends their nationalistic goals in telecommunications 
were enhanced by cultural oneness that allowed for common vision. "It 
is because we are a heterogeneous society that we are strong," Markey 
said. "I believe what separates us from the Japanese is that we are right 
about this, and they are wrong."

Markey said it has taken a while to bring his view of 
telecommunications forward. "If you're right too soon, it can be a 
problem," he admitted. He called on industry to tackle key issue of 
pressing FCC to expand interconnection opportunities whenever 
possible, and he challenged Clinton Administration to develop 
infrastructure necessary to ensure that interoperable networks are 
developed. Perhaps, he said, that might mean funding pilot projects, 
not entire systems. "Let's look at the issue from the user's point of view, 
and that might mean not bringing fiber to his door, but it would mean 
developing a superior information network. That's really what this is 
all about."

Panel on telecommunications providers at symposium debated 
whether current atmosphere amounted to evolution or revolution in 
industry. MCI Senior Vp-Network Engineering Lawrence Bowman, 
who sees "revolution," said it's up to carriers to build and operate 
information network, provide switching and transport technology and 
deliver product to users, with govt. focusing on fostering competitive 
atmosphere and stimulating information technologies of future. "We 
really do need a coordinated effort between government, 
telecommunications, computer providers, entertainment and 
information services. We don't need 13,000 different companies trying 
to build solutions to our problems."

Calling environment evolutionary, Ameritech Services Technology 
Management Vp Joel Engel said even Personal Communication 
Systems will link to existing switched network. Former FCC Chmn. 
Richard Wiley, speaking as chmn. of FCC Advisory Committee on 
Advanced TV, said broadcasters have 2 basic problems: They need to 
open up more channels of operation and they need higher quality 
signals in radio and TV. "There is some cause for optimism," he said, 
saying future is rooted in regulatory changes in FCC limitations on 
ownership and introduction of such technologies as cable radio, 
satellite digital audio broadcasting, AM stereo. Michele Farquhar, vp-
law and regulatory policy, Cellular Telecommunications Industry 
Assn., said trend is toward wireless in virtually all aspects of wireline 
systems, at least at last stage of communications to user.

