HEADLINE: Technology; Wrestling Over the Key to the Codes

BYLINE: By JOHN MARKOFF

DATELINE: MOUNTAIN VIEW, Calif.

BODY:

In the obscure world of computer cryptography, there may be no more 
self-consciously ornery group of coders than the Cypherpunks, an 
alliance of some of Silicon Valley's best programmers and hardware 
designers, who preach absolute privacy in the information age.

The Cypherpunks, who often communicate among themselves by 
electronic mail protected with an encryption system popular in the 
computing underground, feel certain about one thing: The 
Government should not be creating a national encoding standard, as 
the Clinton Administration has recently proposed.

"The Government proposal suppresses secure cryptography and 
replaces it with false cryptography," said Eric Hughes, an independent 
software designer in Silicon Valley who is one of the founders of the 
Cypherpunks.

The Administration wants to establish a national voice and data 
encryption standard as a compromise between those like the 
Cypherpunks who advocate absolute electronic privacy and Federal 
bodies like the National Security Agency and the F.B.I., who insist on 
the need for law enforcement agencies to be able to monitor electronic 
communications.

The proposed standard, designed by the N.S.A., would permit F.B.I. 
and other law enforcement officials to decode encrypted data when 
they have a legal warrant to wiretap.

Because the N.S.A. design would remain a classified secret, sealed in 
tamper-proof computer chips, the system's underlying technology 
would not be subject to review and testing by civilian computer 
experts. That raises deep suspicion among folks like the Cypherpunks, 
whose views on computer privacy have spawned an electronic 
pamphlet, "The Crypto Anarchist Manifesto," which espouses privacy 
without government interference, and ends with a passionate call to 
action: "Arise. You have nothing to lose but your barbed-wire fences."

Even more low-key civilian computer experts harbor suspicions that 
the N.S.A. may have built a secret "trapdoor" into the encryption 
system that could allow unauthorized surveillance -- a fear that the 
N.S.A. insists is groundless.

Civilian computer experts also say that their inability to test the inner 
workings of the technology gives them little certainty that the 
encryption system is secure enough to defeat the efforts of the legion 
hackers who will surely try to break the code.

The controversy is raising public awareness of cryptography, an arcane 
field of mathematics that researchers describe as "the art or science of 
hiding information in plain sight." Modern cryptography works by 
tranforming data into a form unreadable by anyone who does not hold 
the secret decryption key.

For centuries -- until the recent widespread availability of personal 
computers -- secret codes have largely remained the province of spies 
and military men. Even today, American-made hardware or software 
containing advanced coding technology is treated as restricted 
technology by the Government. Along with weapons systems like jet 
fighters and tanks, encryption hardware and software can't be 
exported without a license from the State Department.

THE computer and communications industries, however, have 
increasingly come to view cryptography not as a national-security 
issue but as an essential ingredient in the multitude of new data and 
voice services envisioned as part of the emerging national data 
highway.

With more and more of the nation's personal and private business 
existing as digital information that can be transmitted -- and copied -- 
in nanoseconds, there is a growing need to safeguard such 
information, be it a doctor's cellular phone call to a patient or a 
corporation's computer files on its latest takeover target.

"Electronic communication will be the fabric of tomorrow's society, 
and we will have daily interaction with intimates that we can only 
rarely afford to visit in person," said Whitfield Diffie, a computer 
researcher at Sun Microsystems and one of the nation's leading 
cryptographers. "By codifying the Government's power to spy 
invisibly on these contacts, we take a giant step toward a world in 
which privacy belongs only to the wealthy, the powerful and, perhaps, 
the criminals."

Members of Congress, including Edward J. Markey, the Massachusetts 
Democrat who is chairman of the House telecommunications 
subcommittee, have asked how the Government can expect its plan to 
work unless it simultaneously bans all other use of cryptography 
beyond the officially sanctioned scheme, known as the

Clipper Chip.  Without such a law, anyone who wants to hide data 
from law enforcement investigators could simply send a stream of 
already coded information through the Clipper system -- effectively 
making it undecipherable to even the Government's most powerful 
computers. But at a briefing held in Washington last month after the 
announcement of the Clipper program, Government officials said they 
had no plans to outlaw competing cryptography systems.

PRESIDENT CLINTON has ordered a classified review of the nation's 
cryptography policies, scheduled to be complete this summer. The 
outcome of the review is likely to determine whether the Government 
pushes to adopt Clipper for voice communications, and a companion 
system known as Capstone for computer traffic, as national standards. 
Rather than using coercion, the Government apparently hopes that 
widespread adoption by Federal agencies -- a lucrative market for 
computer and communications companies -- and the potential for 
allowing general export of products based on Clipper or Capstone 
would motivate industry to adopt the technology as a de facto 
standard.

Modern cryptographic techniques fall into several different categories. 
Traditionally, coding schemes had required that both the sender and 
the receiver of a message have access to the same secret key. The key is 
typically a lengthy number that can be plugged into the coding 
algorithm -- the mathematical instructions that "scramble" the 
information into code form. But sending the key to a person to whom 
one then intends to send a coded message is not only cumbersome; it 
risks letting the key fall into the wrong hands.

Seeking to avoid this single-key problem, Mr. Diffie and Martin 
Hellman, a Stanford University computer scientist, invented "public 
key" cryptography in 1976. In their system, which has found its way 
into various encryption programs now on the market, every person 
gets a pair of keys, one public and one private. The public keys are 
published or otherwise disseminated, just as telephone numbers are. 
But the private keys are closely guarded secrets. To transmit a secret 
message, the sender encodes it with the receiver's public key. When the 
receiver obtains the scrambled message it can be read only with the 
correct private key.

The advantage of the Diffie-Hellman public key scheme is that it 
eliminates the need for the sender and receiver of a message to share 
secret information -- the key -- before communicating.

THAT has been the principal limitation of the Data Encryption 
Standard, which for the past 15 years has been the national 
cryptographic standard but is seen as too cumbersome for modern 
electronic commerce.

D.E.S. was originally developed during the 1960's by I.B.M. researchers 
and then modified by the N.S.A. There has been endless speculation 
that there is a flaw or trapdoor in the standard that makes possible 
unauthorized decoding of information encrypted with D.E.S. But the 
D.E.S. formula is publicly available, and despite years of probing by 
some of the world's best researchers, no practical weakness has ever 
been identified. It has been suspected, however, that the N.S.A. or 
anyone else putting a supercomputer to the task of trying endless 
combinations of keys can unlock a D.E.S.-encoded message in a matter 
or hours.

N.S.A. officials insist their new system has no known built-in weakness 
and researchers at the National Institute of Standards and Technology 
say that is because the Clipper key is 80 computer bits of information 
long, compared with 56 bits for D.E.S. The new algorithm is 16 million 
times as secure.

But public cryptography experts like Mr. Diffie say they may never 
share that certainty, because the Government will not let the algorithm 
be examined by anyone who does not have a national security 
clearance. And Mr. Diffie and many of his industry colleagues are 
unwilling to obtain such clearance because they believe it would 
compromise their ability to perform independent research.

And so cryptographic renegades like the Cypherpunks will probably 
stick with their own coding systems. The one currently in vogue is 
PGP, for pretty good privacy. Designed by a Colorado programmer, 
Phil Zimmerman, the PGP public-key coding system is freely available 
on computer networks and can be used on many different types of 
computers and workstations.

But as is so often the case in matters cryptographic, PGP has it own fog 
of controversy. It turns out that Mr. Zimmerman based PGP on public-
key work done by three other American cryptographers: Ronald 
Rivest, Adi Shamir and Leonard Adelman. Their encryption system 
has been patented by RSA Data Security, a Redwood City, Calif., 
software company.

And RSA executives have been threatening to file patent-infringement 
suits against computer operators who make the PGP program 
available on their systems. The fight has spawned copious electronic 
messaging in the computer underground -- mostly written in code, of 
course.

