HEADLINE: Encryption Decision Is Questioned

SERIES: Occasional

BYLINE: John Burgess, Washington Post Staff Writer

BODY:

More than 30 major electronics companies and trade associations have 
written President Clinton, calling into question a White House plan on 
how to guard electronic communications against illegal monitoring.

The group requested that the White House first hold extensive 
discussions about government-developed scrambling technology that 
the administration wants the industry to adopt wholesale.

The White House plan proposes that industry agree to base encryption 
equipment sold to the public on special government-designed 
electronic chips. Dubbed " clipper chips, " they would be installed in 
phones and other communications devices.

Advocates say the technology would protect the public against illegal 
cracking of scrambled calls and fax and computer data transmissions. 
At the same time, it would give police and intelligence agencies that 
obtained court orders the power to decode such communications.

Since the White House proposed the plan three weeks ago, many in the 
computer and communications industries have responded with 
skepticism. Critics wonder how good the secret government 
technology really would be and worry that agencies might abuse it to 
tap calls without court orders.

"We're trying to slow down the administration and have a public 
dialogue," said Jerry Berman, executive director of the Electronic 
Frontier Foundation, which circulated the letter.

The letter does not specifically condemn the plan. But it said that 
"while we recognize the importance of authorized national security 
and law enforcement needs, we believe that there are fundamental 
privacy and other constitutional rights that must be taken into 
account."

Mat Heyman, a spokesman for the National Institute of Standards and 
Technology, an agency that would help implement the plan, said the 
letter hadn't been examined yet. But he noted that "the president early 
on has said that he wants there to be close consultation with the 
industry."

Among the companies signing were International Business Machines 
Corp., AT&T, Lotus Development Corp., Microsoft Corp., McCaw 
Cellular Communications Corp. and MCI Communications Corp. The 
American Civil Liberties Union also took part.

AT&T, which has said it plans to produce  clipper chips,  said it is only 
seeking clarification and does not oppose the idea.

The White House plan grew from federal concern that private 
scrambling technology is advancing so rapidly that government could 
find itself unable to tap the communications of criminals or terrorists.

The  clipper chips  would scramble data to levels that would be 
virtually uncrackable, the White House says. The communications 
could be unscrambled, however, by technicians possessing a unique 
pair of numeric "keys" associated with each chip.

To keep the keys secret, one would be entrusted to one government 
agency, the other to a second agency. They would be released only to 
government authorities who obtained court orders.

